A||||20||Ken Wittlief||New Fiero owner is on the road!||||1||
Z||000000||Ken Wittlief||05-01-2001||09:53 AM||||I drove my fiero home yesterday. I wish I could say it was a joy. I knew the car was gonna need work, in fact I wanted a 'project car' so my 15yr old son and I could work on it together, and he would learn the hows and whys of auto mechanics, and hopefully develope a deep respect for a fine machine before he gets his learners permit in October.<P> Well I got what I wanted, a project car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Its an 85 SE V6 auto w/ 107k miles. A one owner car (machinist from Ford plant, now retired!) It looks like he never messed with anything.<P>has AC, PW, removeable sunroof, great stereo with the speakers in the headrests, the interior is excellent. New Michelin tires all around (I was impressed by that - owner appreciated quality).<P>He stuck to his guns, and I paid $1500. It has not been driven for the last two years.<P>Okay, now the project stuff:<P>1. the silver paint on the hood and tops of the front fenders is oxidized, so there is a paint job in my future.<P>2. It idles fast and I checked the codes, got a 33 (map sensor). dont see any loose vacuum hoses any where, so i guess it could be the sensor. How much am I looking at for a new one?<P>3. The brakes are original, and the front drivers side is hanging up. Im am sure I can loosen it up when I replace the pads, but for now, if I step on the brakes hard I have to get out and tap the caliper from the inside to get it to let go.<P>4. The oil pan leaks. When I drove it home (90 miles) I stopped after 20 miles just to take a look at everything and saw this drip drip drip. I kept it topped off and got home without it dropping below the add mark. After jacking the car up I discovered a pin hole about 3 inches up the side of the oil pan, rusty. I patched it with JB Weld expoxy, a 2 by 3" piece of alum sheet metal, and packing tape over the whole thing to hold it in place while the epoxy set up (this is one for Paul Harvey!).  Its just dripping a little bit now, will have to check it out again tonight, but I think it will do until I can get a new oil pan. Has anyone else ever had an oil pan rust out like that?<P>Is it gonna be a big deal to replace the oil pan? It looks like an exhaust pipe runs under it, so maybe that pipe has to come off first? Or can the oil pan be coaxed around it and replaced?<P>BTW between the oil leak (knowing about the early V6s and the engine fires) and the brake pad hanging up, smelling bad for a while and making the car pull to the left, and the general uneasyness of driving a car you are not use to, it made the drive home an 'adventure'.<P>5. The owner told me he did NOT get the recall work done, cause he didnt like what they were gonna do to it (I think the 85 V6 recall was to add heat sheilds to the cat?). Since the car has survived 107k miles Im not too worried about this, but I will look into it.<P>6. Is there anyway of knowing when the CAT needs replacing (or gutting)? The car is idleing fast, the plugs do carbon up, and it hesitates sometimes when taking off from a stop. I know I have a MAP sensor code, so I dont know how much to atribute to that. Isnt 107k miles pretty much the life expectency of a CAT anyway. I know when they clog up the car will feel, well.. backed up, but since I have never driven one of these I have no reference point.<P>7. Is the radiator fan suppose to run when you are driving the car, or only when you are stuck in traffic and the engine temp gets to a certain point? My dash temp gauge stayed halfway between 100 and 220 (I have no idea what temp that is suppose to be, 160?! are the gauge markings linear? It seems a little cool actually for 70mph highway driving? I was wondering if the gauge was working, but it does start off at the lower limit when the car is cold, so I guess it works. <P>I think maybe the theromostat has been removed? I would expect the car to hit 180 or 200 and stay there. having it run too cool is not good either, and engine will run rich then and tend to carbon up. <P>Is there any other quick check to tell if the engine is overheating. For example, on an aircooled VW, if you cant pull the dipstick handle and hold it in your hand, then its overheating. Any such 'sanity checks' for fireo engine temp?<P>8. I think my heater core is oozing a bit. catching a coolant smell inside the car every once in a while. I turned the heat slider up, and put the defrosters on, and its not real noticable. But after I turned them back off, a few minutes later I noticed a wisp of smoke or steam came out of the defroster vent (freaked me out for a second). If I keep the heater temp slider all the way on cool, does that block the coolant from the heater core, or does it only deflect the airflow around it? Its summertime so I can always bypass the core for now with the hoses if I have to.<P>Also, is there any reason to replace the oxygen sensor if you dont get a code for it? I think the map sensor and maybe a clogged CAT might be the reason for the carbon on the plugs, so I plan on going after those things first. But I have the general impression that oxygen sensors usually dont last over 100k miles.<P>My first milestone will be to get the car inspected. I have a ten day temp sticker on it. It think brake pads on the front, and loosening up the eBrake cables should be all it needs.<P>then I plan on pulling the plugs, doing a compression test, a vacuum test, checking the timing, and poking my nose into every cable and hose I can find to make sure there is nothing loose anywhere.<P>On the timing, does the ECM set that automatically? Can the timing be off on a Fiero? Is the adjustment critical like it is with a vacuum/ centrifical advance distributor and points? Im am pretty sure the timing has to be set with the engine at 600 or 900 RPM, yes? So if its idleing fast I have to fix that first before I can check the timing? Or could the timing being off cause the fast idle?<P>I have mentioned in other posts that I work 3 miles from home, and this is our third car (for 3 drivers), so if I have to putter and limp around in it for a while that is not a big deal. I do want to get it running (and stopping) nicely before I let wife or 18yr old daughter drive it. (Wife is not thrilled with the car so far, will need some positive behind-the-wheel time before she comes around).<P>  I did have the privilige of being the first person to drive the car with the sunroof off! the first owner told me he never bothered to take it off, too much work.<P>  In spite of all these minor things that need work, i think the car is great! Will have to clear out space in the garage so I can leave the sunroof in the trunk most of the time.<P>  guess I will have to spring for the Chilton manual eventaully. Got one from the library for now. Wonder how many times they will let me renew it?<P>  cant wait for lunch time so I can zip 3 miles home and get a peanut butter sandwich!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cheever2||05-01-2001||10:08 AM||||Wow, that was long.  Good questions, however.<P>You definitely need a heater core replacement.  Those are the classic symptoms.  I hope your hands aren't as big as mine (and mine aren't all that big).  I'm ready to put a chair to the heads of the people who told me it's an easy do-it-yourself swap.  But that's where my own 15 year old son was a big help.  He's skinny.  Follow TheOgre's instructions in The Cave, they work great.<P>Can't help you with the other stuff, sorry.  My wife might be a little happier if she got to drive the car too, but mostly I need to buy some furniture instead of cars & parts.<BR>||||reg||4
Z||000002||Old Lar||05-01-2001||10:34 AM||||Well Ken, I've got a Haynes Manual.  My 87 GT runs cool according to the temp guage in the car,  It started when I replace the stock exhaust and cat.  I replaced the 195 degree thermostat and it made no difference, but the car runs great.  I figured that the cat replacement ($100 at NAPA) was small expense after 14 years.<P>My fan runs when the temp goes up or the AC is on, otherwise its not on.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ferg||05-01-2001||11:23 AM||||You can put the sunroof up front, it lays on top of the spare tire. Those little brackets with the padding on it are for the sunroof. If you have the wind deflector, you'll have to remove that to put the sunroof up front.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ken Wittlief||05-01-2001||11:32 AM||||I got the sunroof in the trunk and the air deflector up on the roof. We get dew up here in NY at night. If I can park the car in the garage then I can leave the sunroof in the trunk until I need to drive in the rain.<P>  Would be cool if I could manage to keep it off for a month or more at a time  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ken Wittlief||05-01-2001||11:34 AM||||Oh I see what you are saying now. Yes, the FRONT trunk! Thats where I have it, not in the back trunk.<P>  I gotta get the terminology down right!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Bob Englert||05-01-2001||12:00 PM||||Ken:<P>Welcome to the wonderful and sometimes frustrating world of Fiero ownership!<P>Oil pan rusting is not uncommon if the car has been driven in a heavy salt area.  <P>Personally, I would suggest that you do the following:<P>1) Get a Haynes manual - I find it much better than the Chiltons for the typical do-it-yourself work.  Consider getting a Helms factory manual for your year Fiero on eBay.<P>2) Change all fluids and filters (oil, air, fuel), coolant, flush the brake lines, transmission fluid, and clutch hydraulics (if equipped).<P>3) Do a complete tuneup (cap rotor, plugs, ignition wires).<P>4) Check condition of the radiator hoses and belts carefully.<P>5) Check the rear brakes and the e-brake to see that they are working.  The e-brake often fails do to lack of use due to frozen mechanism or cables.  Always use the e-brake as it help to maintain the rear calipers in adjustment.  <P>6) 84-87 calipers are fairly inexpensive.  Take the time to make sure the brakes are done right, since the Fiero brakes were never noted to be high performance in the first place.<P>7) Consider replacing all vac lines.  If the car has sat for a long time they are probably dry rotted and will continue to crack and leak as you start driving the car regularly.<P>8) I would suggest replacing the MAP sensor if the code is present and also the O2 sensor as part of your tuneup.<P>9) All Fieros except the 87-88 4 cyl had a distributor and you need to set the base timing with the ECM jumpered out.  This is done be getting to the diagnostic connector under the cigar lighter plate between the seats.  Most parts houses such as AutoZone sell a code reader clip tool for about $1.  This needs to be in to set the base timing.  The Haynes manual should have instructions.<P>10) Check the condition of the dogbone and the motor and transmission mounts carefully.  Replace if needed, particularly the dogbone if it is a stock rubber bushing one.<P>Have fun! and welcome....<P><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000007||84Bill||05-01-2001||12:12 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>I can leave the sunroof in the trunk until I need to drive in the rain.<P>  Would be cool if I could manage to keep it off for a month or more at a time   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well you could drive really really fast and not stop if it rains that way you wont get wet  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||10
Z||000008||Ken Wittlief||05-01-2001||01:20 PM||||I went to the local autoparts store to get a MAP sensor during lunch, unhooked the battery so it would reset, took the old sensor off and the new one wouldnt mate with the connector, it was keyed differently.<P>  Took it back inside, got a refund, put the old sensor back in, hooked up the battery, figured I head to the pontiac dealer.<P>  stated the car up and guess what? No Check Engine light! Car idles at 800 rpm in drive now!!!<P>   I think maybe just the connector was corroded or dirty, the ECM thinks the MAP sensor is okay now.<P>  engine is still a little sluggish, I guess the CAT is next project for the drivetrain.<P>  But first gotta do the front brakes and get the rear ones to adjust correctly. The previous owner never used the Ebrake, which could be a good thing, if you never use it then maybe the insides of the cables never get water and salt in there.<P>   We'll see.<P>  taking off from a red lite on a left turn I spun the tires all the way around the corner - that little thing has some guts to it.<P>   So how do I get this stupid grin off my face? ||||reg||1
Z||000009||84Bill||05-01-2001||01:30 PM||||Hey ken check out this thread. The grin will remain long after your gone<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/007155.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/007155.html</A> ||||reg||12
Z||000010||Ken Wittlief||05-01-2001||01:50 PM||||Lets see, how many warning signs are there:<P>Do you have to drive your Fiero first thing in the morning?<P>Do you ever wake up in the morning and cant remember all the places you drove the day before?<P>Has your Fiero ever caused problems with your family?<P>Do you lie to your loved ones about how much you drive your Fiero, or about how many you have?<P>Do you ever drive alone?<P>When you are at a party, do you feel more comfortable if you are sitting in a Fiero?<P>Have you ever missed work so you could be out driving?<P>Are you eyes bloodshot from sticking your head out of the sunroof at 90mph?<P>Is the top of your head sunburned?<P>Have you ever driven around a corner so fast you felt like you were out of control?<P>Do you drive your Fiero more than 3 times a day?<P>Do you make up reasons to go places, just so you can drive your Fiero?<P>Do you hang around online Fiero Fourms everyday - have you posted more than 50 messages on one?<P>If you answered any of these questions you may be a Fieroholic!||||reg||1
Z||000011||84Bill||05-01-2001||02:02 PM||||HAHAHA!! yeah, yeah, yeah, yeaaah, yep, yup, uhhh huh, yes, yes, uhhhhh yep, definatly, all the time, yep, 2 times on Sunday, <P><BR>Welcome to the Club KEN!!<BR>Enjoy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||Cheever2||05-01-2001||02:52 PM||||Ken, that was so true it's, er, scary...(no that's not right), sad...(that's not it either), amazing...(well sort of), oh what the heck - COWABUNGA!<P>Fiero on, dude!<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000013||rogergarrison||05-01-2001||03:23 PM||||the map sensor are color coded. you have to use one with same color (ie, orange,red,black,green,blue) The GM dealers only have the black one and it wont work unless the orig is black-no matter what they tell you. I found the whole color code group at Autozone.<P>As for your other problems, most are relative to the car sitting for a long time not running, like the calipers sticking, rusty oil pan. maybe a bunch of spots going in exhaust manifold too. Change all your fluids like said already, Open the cap at radiator hose by the intake manifold. thats where the thermostat is, just pull out the little handle. Replace it if there is one or not, there cheap. Heater core rust from sitting. Yes the fan is supposed to come on any time the water temp reaches a predetermined point. Mine can come on in traffic or on interstate. Always comes on when you turn on AC.<P>Welcome to forum, read and ask, youll learn lots here.<P>Roger<P><BR>UOTE]Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>I went to the local autoparts store to get a MAP sensor during lunch, unhooked the battery so it would reset, took the old sensor off and the new one wouldnt mate with the connector, it was keyed differently.<P>  Took it back inside, got a refund, put the old sensor back in, hooked up the battery, figured I head to the pontiac dealer.<P>  stated the car up and guess what? No Check Engine light! Car idles at 800 rpm in drive now!!!<P>   I think maybe just the connector was corroded or dirty, the ECM thinks the MAP sensor is okay now.<P>  engine is still a little sluggish, I guess the CAT is next project for the drivetrain.<P>  But first gotta do the front brakes and get the rear ones to adjust correctly. The previous owner never used the Ebrake, which could be a good thing, if you never use it then maybe the insides of the cables never get water and salt in there.<P>   We'll see.<P>  taking off from a red lite on a left turn I spun the tires all the way around the corner - that little thing has some guts to it.<P>   So how do I get this stupid grin off my face? </B>[/QUOTE]<P><P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Ken Wittlief||05-01-2001||03:37 PM||||the MAP sensor that is in the car is black, the the parts store (not a GM dealer) had one that was black too, and his book said it was right for an 85 V6, but the connector has little keys on it and they did not match.<P> I noticed the female connector on the MAP sensor is green, and the rest is black.<P>But I think it might be working now, so I will wait and see if I get another check engine light. ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Ken Wittlief||05-02-2001||01:17 PM||||Oh Sweet Mother of Blessed Acceration<P>Thank you for Favors received!!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I would never expect the timing on an electronic ignition engine to be off, but after reading some of the archived posts on this forum I decided to check mine, just on a whim!<P>Was at 4°, is now at 10°.<P> Whoa baby!!! Thats the great thing about buying a project car, its just keeps getting better and better!<P>  Maybe my mpg will get up above 13 now. I have a new oxygen sensor to put in when I get the chance (107k miles / $20 at PartsPlus, i figure, why not?).<P>  cant wait to fix the brake so it stops hanging, so I can 'really' drive the car. (the brakes work ok, but if I lean on them good the one sticks and I have to get out and wack the caliper with a hammer, or nail a good bump in the road).||||reg||1
Z||000016||rogergarrison||05-02-2001||01:47 PM||||point of clarification= all the map sensors themselves are black, the connector color, in your case green should match the plug where it connects under /inside the sensor. if you take the sensor off and look into the connector your socket should be green also.Each of the colors operates a little differently and each is keyed different so the wrong one cant be installed. GM no longer has any except the black coded one.<P>Id also change oil and filter right away and add a can of cylinder top oil, like Marvel Mystery Oil, that will free up any sticky valves, lifters, rings that have stuck due to not running.<P>   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>the MAP sensor that is in the car is black, the the parts store (not a GM dealer) had one that was black too, and his book said it was right for an 85 V6, but the connector has little keys on it and they did not match.<P> I noticed the female connector on the MAP sensor is green, and the rest is black.<P>But I think it might be working now, so I will wait and see if I get another check engine light. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 05-02-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 05-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||Ken Wittlief||05-02-2001||02:07 PM||||GM no longer has any except the black connector ones? Yikes! The connector on mind is green.<P>   Does that mean when the map sensor goes I will have to drive around in limp-home mode for the rest of the cars life? Did someone buy up the rest of GMs stock of the rest of them? I may end up having to take the thing apart someday and making a home-made replacement for it (how hard can it be, vacuum on one end, three wires on the other).<P>  Somebody has to still make replacements for these things somewhere?!||||reg||1
Z||000018||rogergarrison||05-02-2001||10:27 PM||||Autozone has all the codes available, sure other aftermarket parts stores do to. If you cant find one, youll have to call one of the vendors in the forum like the Fiero Store.<P>Manufactures usually only make replacement parts for an average of 8 years after model year. After that you have to do a little digging, lots of aftermarket stuff around. Gotta remember they havent made our great little cars for almost 15 years. One reason ones found in junkyards are usually gold mines and get picked clean pretty fast.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 05-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||Ken Wittlief||05-03-2001||02:03 PM||||The saga continues:<P>I checked my temp sensor and found it is open. It should read 440 ohms at 160°F, so I bypassed it to ground with a 440 ohm resistor, and my check engine code check finally says I'm running closed loop now!<P>yea!<P>But its still running rich, and still stumbles from a redlight, so I will check the rest of the sensors with a voltmeter. (That works very nicely BTW, as outlined on the Fiero Online Service Guide).<P>Im gonna get 25mph highway out of this thing if I have to rebuild the entire car!||||reg||1
Z||000020||Ken Wittlief||05-03-2001||02:04 PM||||oops! <P>make that 25mpg highway. Cruises at 75mph very nicely already.||||reg||1
